    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Electoral participation in Iran includes voting, abstention, campaign involvement, and arguments over whether elections can produce change. Reported turnout measures participation in a particular contest, but cannot alone establish voters’ motives or the fairness of the electoral process.",
    "tags": ["Voter turnout", "Political participation", "Elections", "Abstention", "Electoral data"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:56:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:56:21Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Electoral Participation in Iran: Turnout, Abstention and Interpretation",
      "description": "Explore reported presidential turnout in Iran, the distinction between election rounds, and the limits of interpreting voting and abstention."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "participation-and-measurement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What turnout measures",
        "content": "Turnout describes how many eligible people participate in a specified election or round. It should be distinguished from a candidate’s share of votes: winning a majority of ballots does not mean winning the support of a majority of all eligible citizens. The Iran Data Portal presents eligible voters, actual voters, and turnout as separate fields, allowing readers to distinguish the electorate from those who voted. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nIran’s results require particular attention to provenance. The portal identifies the Ministry of Interior as the official source of election results and states that Iran does not accept international election monitoring. The observations below are therefore reported electoral statistics, not an independent audit of participation. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "presidential-pattern",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A changing presidential electorate",
        "content": "The selected presidential observations show substantial variation. Reported participation was similar in the elections that returned Hassan Rouhani to the presidency and considerably lower in the subsequent contest won by Ebrahim Raisi. The early presidential election following Raisi’s death then produced markedly different participation levels across its two rounds. The dataset preserves those rounds separately instead of averaging them into a misleading annual figure. [s1][s2][s3][s4]\n\nThese are election observations, not an annual social indicator. A year without a presidential contest does not have zero presidential turnout, and a line drawn through intervening years would suggest measurements that were never made. The accompanying bar chart therefore compares named contests directly.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "turnout-comparison",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "bar",
          "title": "Reported turnout in selected Iranian presidential elections and rounds",
          "dataset_id": "presidential-turnout",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["reported-turnout"],
          "x_axis_label": "Presidential election or round",
          "y_axis_label": "Eligible voters participating (%)",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "Reported turnout was above 70% in the two earliest contests shown, below 50% in the later observations, and higher in the 2024 runoff than in that election’s first round. These figures describe reported participation rather than independently verified voter motivation."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "motives-and-choice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Voting and abstention carry different meanings",
        "content": "Participation is politically contested because voters disagree about what an election can achieve. International Crisis Group’s research on the 2024 presidential race documented both rejection of participation and arguments for voting to prevent a less desirable outcome. Some prominent figures who had abstained in the first round subsequently supported Masoud Pezeshkian against Saeed Jalili in the runoff. This illustrates how a person’s decision can change with the available choice. It does not establish that every additional voter shared that motivation. [s4]\n\nCandidate access also shapes the context in which participation occurs. The House of Commons Library’s review of the March 2024 parliamentary election described exclusions of reformist and moderate candidates, official appeals for turnout, and opposition boycott calls. A turnout figure should consequently be read alongside information about who could stand and campaign. Neither a high rate by itself proves open competition nor a low rate identifies every non-voter as an organized boycotter. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "geography-and-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "National totals have limits",
        "content": "Election data can support more detailed geographical inquiry, but the available measures must match the question. The portal’s presidential tables include provincial voting results; these reveal where ballots were cast and how candidates performed. Calculating provincial turnout additionally requires a corresponding eligible electorate. Candidate vote shares cannot substitute for that denominator. [s2]\n\nA useful reading of participation therefore combines the contest, round, reporting source, denominator, and political setting. The national series here offers a documented starting point. It does not measure campaign activity, civic engagement outside elections, or the views of citizens who did not vote.",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [
