  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-8-7-6",
    "page_path": "/explore/governance/electoral-system/election-administration/",
    "name": "Election Administration",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/governance/electoral-system/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iranian election administration combines Ministry of Interior implementation with separate supervisory institutions. National elections and local council elections share administrative machinery but follow different supervisory arrangements.",
    "tags": ["Election administration", "Ministry of Interior", "Electoral supervision", "Polling stations", "Local government"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:56:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:56:21Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Who Runs Iran’s Elections? Administration and Supervision",
      "description": "Understand the Ministry of Interior’s election role, Guardian Council supervision, local council oversight, and polling-station responsibilities."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "execution-and-supervision",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Running an election and supervising it",
        "content": "Iran’s electoral machinery has distinct executive and supervisory components. The Ministry of Interior administers elections and publishes official results, while other bodies exercise election-specific supervisory authority. The Iran Data Portal identifies the ministry as the official results source and notes the absence of accepted international election monitoring. Domestic supervision should therefore be distinguished from independent international observation. [s1]\n\nFor presidential elections, the inspected supervision law establishes a central supervisory board selected by the Guardian Council. Its remit covers election stages, Ministry of Interior actions, executive bodies, and questions affecting validity. It also provides for supervisors at county level. These arrangements place supervision alongside implementation rather than turning the supervisory board into the organizer of every polling operation. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "national-supervision",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "National supervisory chains",
        "content": "Parliamentary supervision has its own statutory hierarchy. The inspected law describes central, provincial, and electoral-district supervisory bodies, with responsibilities for reviewing election conduct and complaints. Its escalation provisions matter: evidence supporting suspension or annulment is forwarded to the Guardian Council, which holds the final decision under the cited law. [s3]\n\nPresidential supervision similarly distinguishes identifying a problem from deciding the election’s validity. Supervisors report infringements to executive officials for corrective action, and unresolved issues can reach the Guardian Council. The presidential law reserves suspension or annulment decisions to that council. These provisions establish formal responsibilities; they do not independently demonstrate how effectively a particular complaint was investigated. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "local-administration",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Local councils follow a separate arrangement",
        "content": "Local council elections use a different supervisory structure. The inspected council-election regulations provide for a central supervisory body elected by parliament from its members and for subordinate territorial supervisory bodies. They also distinguish the polling-station representative responsible for executive decisions from the station supervisor responsible for supervision. Shared premises or simultaneous elections do not erase those different legal functions. [s4]\n\nThe consolidated council law requires executive bodies to keep supervisory bodies informed and provide copies of their records. It separately authorizes Ministry of Interior inspections. These provisions create channels for information and scrutiny within the domestic administration, while leaving the distinction between execution and supervision intact. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "council-relationships",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Selected relationships in local council election administration",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  P[\"Parliament\"] -->|\"Elects members of\"| C[\"Central council-election supervisory body\"]\n  C -->|\"Establishes provincial supervision\"| S[\"Territorial supervisory bodies\"]\n  M[\"Ministry of Interior\"] -->|\"Issues election instructions\"| G[\"County and district governors\"]\n  G -->|\"Chairs corresponding bodies\"| E[\"Local executive bodies\"]\n  E -->|\"Provides election records to\"| S\n  S -->|\"Reports defects for correction\"| G\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram separates the parliamentary supervisory chain from Ministry of Interior implementation. It shows selected legal relationships for local council elections, not the supervisory arrangement for presidential or parliamentary elections.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "polling-logistics",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The practical work behind polling",
        "content": "The council-election regulations make administration concrete: local executive arrangements determine polling locations, arrange staffing and training, prepare premises, and distribute ballots and equipment. They expressly address advance delivery to remote or difficult-to-access areas. Such provisions show why election administration involves geographical and logistical planning as well as legal decisions. A requirement to provide equipment, however, is not evidence that every location received it on time. [s4]\n\nThe central distinction for readers is between an announced result, its supporting administrative record, and a supervisory decision concerning validity. The documented framework explains who is meant to perform each function. Evaluating an individual election additionally requires contemporaneous records of implementation, complaints, and their resolution; those outcomes cannot be inferred from institutional design alone.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
